    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, the single `@Test` class is the whole suite; the other two files are support classes).\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133), keeping structural impact low by editing small, cohesive units:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 swapped the `membershipTier` (string) property for `membershipLevel` (integer, `minimum: 1`, `maximum: 3`), which regenerates `OwnerDto`. This removes `membershipTier` from the response.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 retargeted the mapping to `membershipLevel` and replaced the `membershipTier` default method with a small `membershipLevel` method: start at 1, `+1` for an email, `+1` when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3 (level 4 left reserved for tenure). CC \u2248 3.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 the create audit line now records `membershipLevel={}` (via `ownerMapper.membershipLevel(owner)`) instead of the tier, per the spec.\n\nThe new test `coreNumericLevel` expects level 3 (base 1 + email + zero namesakes, capped) and passes; the audit output confirms `membershipLevel=3`.\n\nOne note on interpretation: the existing audit line recorded `id/customerCode/registrationDate`, not the tier, so there was no literal tier token to swap. I read the spec's \"the create audit line must record the level\" as a requirement and added `membershipLevel` to that line. If you'd prefer the audit left untouched, that one-line change is easy to revert.",
